## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design privacy-aware, tenant-scoped analytics with late-arriving data. Emphasizes incremental logic, governance, and lineage.

## Key Concepts
- Incremental models with composite keys (tenant_id, date, event_type)
- Field-level redaction controlled by privacy_flag via a macro
- Late-arriving data handled through a short lookback MERGE
- Schema drift guards using sources, tests, and snapshots
- Exposures to reflect lineage in dashboards

## Code Example
```sql
-- macros/redact_email.sql
{% macro redact_email(email, privacy_flag) -%}
  {% if privacy_flag -%}
    {{ return('REDACTED') }}
  {% else -%}
    {{ return(email) }}
  {% endif -%}
{% endmacro %}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the redact_email macro across combinations of privacy_flag and null emails?
- How would you scale this approach to thousands of tenants with varying data freshness SLAs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests handling of per-tenant incremental pipelines, simple data freshness logic, and a CI gate without deep complexity.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant incremental models
- Data freshness checks and basic SLA gates
- dbt tests: not_null and unique; backfill strategy
- Lightweight CI integration for production guardrails

## Code Example
```javascript
// sketch of a freshness macro concept (dbt uses SQL/Jinja; shown here for clarity)
function freshness_check(tenant_id, event_date) {
  const maxDate = `SELECT tenant_id, MAX(${event_date}) as max_date FROM {{ this }} GROUP BY tenant_id`;
  // compare to current_date and decide SLA breach
  return `CASE WHEN DATEDIFF(current_date, max_date) > 2 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END`;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to handle partial backfills for previously SLA-compliant tenants?
- What metrics or dashboards would you add to monitor SLA adherence over time?
